      "writtenOn": "2024-01-30T14:11:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Does Zephyr check if pointer parameters are non-null?\n\nThis is a bit more complicated. Marking a function as a `__syscall` does not introduce any checks. API function may be okay with passing NULL as a pointer, it could mean that e.g. the caller don\u0027t need the result.\n\nThe `__syscall` is a information for some Zephyr preprocessing script that it should generate a code to pass arguments from user space to kernel space. Generated code is included in file that has `__syscall` functions (See line 68 in `fingerprint_sim.h` file). The syscall code for `fingerprint_set_state()` (CONFIG_USERSPACE is disabled in our case):\n\n```\nextern void z_impl_fingerprint_set_state(const struct device * dev, struct fingerprint_sensor_state * state);\n\n__pinned_func\nstatic inline void fingerprint_set_state(const struct device * dev, struct fingerprint_sensor_state * state)\n{\n#ifdef CONFIG_USERSPACE\n        if (z_syscall_trap()) {\n                union { uintptr_t x; const struct device * val; } parm0 \u003d { .val \u003d dev };\n                union { uintptr_t x; struct fingerprint_sensor_state * val; } parm1 \u003d { .val \u003d state };\n                (void) arch_syscall_invoke2(parm0.x, parm1.x, K_SYSCALL_FINGERPRINT_SET_STATE);\n                return;\n        }\n#endif\n        compiler_barrier();\n        z_impl_fingerprint_set_state(dev, state);\n}\n```\n\nAs you can see, without `CONFIG_USERSPACE` `z_impl_fingerprint_set_state()` is called directly.\n\nFrom comment https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/issues/21513#issuecomment-606780891:\n\u003e Anything that supports userspace (syscalls) already verifies parameters when actually calling from userspace into kernel mode.\n\nI think Carles was talking about `z_vrfy_*()` functions, which are responsible for verifying data. They are also responsible for mitigating TOCTOU bugs that can occur in SMP systems.\n\nExample verify function:\n```\nstatic inline int z_vrfy_flash_read(const struct device *dev, off_t offset,\n                                    void *data, size_t len)\n{\n        K_OOPS(K_SYSCALL_DRIVER_FLASH(dev, read));\n        K_OOPS(K_SYSCALL_MEMORY_WRITE(data, len));\n        return z_impl_flash_read((const struct device *)dev, offset,\n                                 (void *)data,\n                                 len);\n}\n```\n\nIn this case we check if the driver implements `read` function, later we check if the thread has write permissions to the buffer (because evil task could ask kernel to write data and corrupt some other task - kernel can write everywhere). This also means that the driver must be trusted, so I\u0027m starting to think that running FPC1025 driver in kernel is a mistake, but again - without CONFIG_USERSPACE, it doesn\u0027t matter really.\n\nhttps://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/kernel/usermode/syscalls.html is a great document that explains syscalls better than me 😊\n\nRegarding checking for non-null parameters - usually in `z_impl_*()` functions we check if the API function we try to call is implemented(*) (but we don\u0027t check if `dev` is non-null) and arguments are checked by the driver.\n\n(*) Of course there are exceptions - SPI API has 3 functions and all 3 must be implemented, so there is no check, we just expect that they are implemented.",
